    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremy-bentham">Jeremy Bentham</a> opened his Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation by placing mankind under two sovereign masters, pain and pleasure, and approved every action, private or governmental, by its tendency to increase or reduce happiness.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a> rejected a purely quantitative measure of utility and held that some pleasures are intrinsically better than others, so that it is better to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> dissatisfied than a fool satisfied.</li><li>Utilitarianism is a cluster of related theories developed over two hundred years, and its supporters have disagreed over act and rule utilitarianism and over total and average utility.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a> believed he was the first to bring the word utilitarian into use, adopting it from a passing expression in John Galt's novel Annals of the Parish (1821), but Bentham had used the word in a letter of 1781.</p></aside>
    </section>

